-- title:
--   Chronologically legal histories
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. Let $\mathrm{legal}(\text{past},r)$ be a relation between lists of records and a new record.
--
--   1. **Sequential history.** The empty list is a history, and if $L$ is a history and $\mathrm{legal}(L,r)$, then $L\,r$ is a history. So every entry was admitted relative to its exact preceding prefix:
--   $$
--   \mathrm{legal}(L_{<i},L_i)\quad\text{for all }i.
--   $$
--   2. **History states.** A list together with a proof that it is a sequential history, with the empty state and the append operation. Appending never changes earlier entries.
